WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.199
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.499 --> 00:00:08.595
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.895 --> 00:00:16.691
Unit six: Literature and Society. In this unit, you will discuss how literature reflects and shapes society.

4
00:00:17.683 --> 00:00:19.068
literary canon.

5
00:00:19.768 --> 00:00:24.519
literary canon means the works considered most important in a literary tradition.

6
00:00:24.819 --> 00:00:28.246
The literary canon has long excluded many voices.

7
00:00:28.946 --> 00:00:32.156
Your turn. Say: literary canon.

8
00:00:34.856 --> 00:00:36.054
literary canon.

9
00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:38.771
postcolonial literature.

10
00:00:39.471 --> 00:00:45.104
postcolonial literature means writing that responds to the experience of colonialism.

11
00:00:45.404 --> 00:00:49.087
Postcolonial literature questions European narratives.

12
00:00:49.787 --> 00:00:53.612
Your turn. Say: postcolonial literature.

13
00:00:56.312 --> 00:00:58.103
postcolonial literature.

14
00:00:59.117 --> 00:01:00.675
magical realism.

15
00:01:01.375 --> 00:01:06.660
magical realism means a style mixing realistic settings with magical events.

16
00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.807
García Márquez is famous for magical realism.

17
00:01:10.507 --> 00:01:14.425
Your turn. Say: magical realism.

18
00:01:17.125 --> 00:01:18.579
magical realism.

19
00:01:19.583 --> 00:01:21.269
unreliable narrator.

20
00:01:21.969 --> 00:01:26.755
unreliable narrator means a storyteller whose account cannot be fully trusted.

21
00:01:27.055 --> 00:01:31.121
The unreliable narrator hides the truth until the final chapter.

22
00:01:31.821 --> 00:01:35.902
Your turn. Say: unreliable narrator.

23
00:01:38.602 --> 00:01:40.230
unreliable narrator.

24
00:01:41.217 --> 00:01:42.752
stream of consciousness.

25
00:01:43.452 --> 00:01:48.911
stream of consciousness means writing that follows a character's continuous flow of thoughts.

26
00:01:49.211 --> 00:01:51.803
Virginia Woolf used stream of consciousness.

27
00:01:52.503 --> 00:01:56.281
Your turn. Say: stream of consciousness.

28
00:01:58.981 --> 00:02:00.598
stream of consciousness.

29
00:02:01.583 --> 00:02:02.677
satire.

30
00:02:03.377 --> 00:02:08.256
satire means using humour or exaggeration to criticise society.

31
00:02:08.556 --> 00:02:11.531
Animal Farm is a political satire.

32
00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:15.580
Your turn. Say: satire.

33
00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:19.269
satire.

34
00:02:20.283 --> 00:02:21.517
bildungsroman.

35
00:02:22.217 --> 00:02:27.153
bildungsroman means a novel about a character's growth from youth to maturity.

36
00:02:27.453 --> 00:02:30.010
Many first novels are a bildungsroman.

37
00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:34.268
Your turn. Say: bildungsroman.

38
00:02:36.968 --> 00:02:38.074
bildungsroman.

39
00:02:39.083 --> 00:02:40.595
dystopian fiction.

40
00:02:41.295 --> 00:02:45.942
dystopian fiction means stories set in nightmarish future societies.

41
00:02:46.242 --> 00:02:49.495
Dystopian fiction often reflects present fears.

42
00:02:50.195 --> 00:02:53.974
Your turn. Say: dystopian fiction.

43
00:02:56.674 --> 00:02:58.116
dystopian fiction.

44
00:02:59.117 --> 00:03:01.279
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:01.579 --> 00:03:05.111
Ms. Sarah, why should we read novels written in other centuries?

46
00:03:06.417 --> 00:03:12.294
Because the stories through which societies imagine themselves reveal what they value and fear.

47
00:03:13.583 --> 00:03:17.162
Like dystopian fiction reflecting fears about surveillance?

48
00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:26.222
Exactly. And postcolonial literature challenges the narrow, largely European literary canon of the past.

49
00:03:27.517 --> 00:03:31.992
My favourite is magical realism. It makes the impossible feel ordinary.

50
00:03:33.283 --> 00:03:38.476
A style that has inspired a whole generation of Southeast Asian writers, in fact.

51
00:03:39.783 --> 00:03:43.864
And I love an unreliable narrator. You never know whom to trust.

52
00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:49.541
Which is, arguably, the best preparation for reading the news.

53
00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.674
Grammar focus: Complex noun phrases.

54
00:03:53.974 --> 00:03:57.634
C2 writing uses complex noun phrases to pack meaning.

55
00:03:57.934 --> 00:04:02.325
The stories through which societies imagine themselves reveal their values.

56
00:04:02.625 --> 00:04:06.935
The narrow, largely European literary canon of the past was challenged.

57
00:04:07.235 --> 00:04:13.704
Complex noun phrases let you express sophisticated ideas compactly; keep the head noun clear.

58
00:04:14.717 --> 00:04:17.903
Now read and listen. Why Literature Matters.

59
00:04:18.203 --> 00:04:23.361
Literature has always been a mirror in which societies examine their hopes and anxieties.

60
00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:31.305
For centuries, the literary canon was dominated by a narrow group of mostly European male writers.

61
00:04:32.617 --> 00:04:39.562
Postcolonial literature, emerging from formerly colonised nations, offered powerful alternative perspectives.

62
00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:47.424
Techniques such as stream of consciousness allowed writers to represent the complexity of inner life.

63
00:04:48.717 --> 00:04:53.642
Satire, from Swift to Orwell, has used exaggeration to expose injustice.

64
00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:59.771
Dystopian fiction imagines futures that warn us about tendencies in the present.

65
00:05:01.083 --> 00:05:06.636
The bildungsroman, meanwhile, traces the painful and rewarding process of growing up.

66
00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:15.429
In an age of short attention spans, the sustained imaginative effort of reading may be more valuable than ever.

67
00:05:16.717 --> 00:05:19.532
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:19.832 --> 00:05:23.689
A storyteller whose account cannot be trusted is an blank.

69
00:05:23.989 --> 00:05:29.070
A, unreliable narrator. B, literary canon. C, satire.

70
00:05:33.083 --> 00:05:35.745
The answer is A: unreliable narrator.

71
00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:39.482
Which is a complex noun phrase?

72
00:05:39.782 --> 00:05:44.376
A, the book. B, the books that shaped modern thought. C, books.

73
00:05:48.383 --> 00:05:51.973
The answer is B: the books that shaped modern thought.

74
00:05:53.483 --> 00:05:56.876
'The world blank which the novel is set' is grim.

75
00:05:57.176 --> 00:06:00.005
A, in. B, on. C, at.

76
00:06:04.017 --> 00:06:06.283
The answer is A: in.

77
00:06:07.783 --> 00:06:09.922
A novel about growing up is a blank.

78
00:06:10.222 --> 00:06:16.558
A, bildungsroman. B, dystopian fiction. C, magical realism.

79
00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:23.142
The answer is A: bildungsroman.

80
00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:27.927
According to the text, what does dystopian fiction do?

81
00:06:28.227 --> 00:06:34.539
A, imagines warning futures. B, describes the past. C, avoids politics.

82
00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:41.560
The answer is A: imagines warning futures.

83
00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:49.301
Now it's your turn. Part one: Which book or story has most influenced your view of the world?

84
00:06:54.601 --> 00:07:01.036
Part two: Describe it using a complex noun phrase and explain its social message.

85
00:07:07.336 --> 00:07:18.313
The novel that most shaped my thinking was Orwell's Animal Farm, a short but devastating political satire. It shows how revolutions intended to free people can produce new tyrannies.

86
00:07:18.613 --> 00:07:20.485
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:21.483 --> 00:07:26.284
Great work today! You learned eight new words and complex noun phrases.

88
00:07:26.584 --> 00:07:30.896
Today's value is creativity. Use your imagination when you play.

89
00:07:31.196 --> 00:07:33.837
See you in Unit seven. Goodbye!

90
00:07:34.137 --> 00:07:36.801
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

